<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Isle of Wight</h1><p class="st-meta">530 CE – 686 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny Jutish kingdom on the Isle of Wight, the last part of England to accept Christianity, ended by the violent conquest of Caedwalla in 686.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> reports that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>, like Kent and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> claims that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynric">Cynric</a> took the island in 530 and gave it to their kinsmen Stuf and Wihtgar, but the story is largely legendary.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Wihtwara lived from farming and the sea under their own kings, sometimes dependents of greater neighbours. In 661 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wulfhere-of-mercia">Wulfhere</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia</a> gave the island to the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelwealh-of-sussex">Aethelwealh</a>. The island was still pagan in the 680s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 686 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedwalla">Caedwalla</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a> conquered the island, killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwald">Arwald</a> and had his two young brothers executed after baptism, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> recounts. The island became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and was converted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid's</a> clergy.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 530 CE – 686 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:70.25%;width:3.10%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>530 CE – 686 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arwald</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglo-Saxon paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wihtwara, Wihtland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=608&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-isle-of-wight" aria-label="Kingdom of the Isle of Wight on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4639 344 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Isle of Wight lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4959 600h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4959 600h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=608&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-isle-of-wight">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

